<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokushima Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a> Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to the former province of Awa, with its capital at Tokushima. It was created in 1871 when the Tokushima domain of the Hachisuka family was abolished, and after a period of merger with neighbouring prefectures in the 1870s it took its present form in 1880. It is known for the Naruto whirlpools and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-odori">Awa Odori</a> festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokushima-prefecture.jpg" alt="Tokushima Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suicasmo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naruto_whirlpools_20170609-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tokushima Prefecture lies on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a> and has an area of 4,146 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ehime-prefecture">Ehime Prefecture</a> to the west and Kōchi Prefecture to the south-west. It faces the Kii Peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> across the Kii Channel, which connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> with the Seto Inland Sea. The Sanuki Mountains run along its northern border, and the Yoshino River flows through the prefecture, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields in its lower valley and along the narrow coastal plains.</p><p>The capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokushima">Tokushima</a>, and other major cities include Anan, Naruto and Yoshinogawa. The Ōnaruto Bridge carries a road across the Naruto Strait to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island">Awaji Island</a>, joining the prefecture to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> and the San'yō Expressway on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the area was known as Awa Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In ancient times Tokushima City belonged to a district known as Myōdō-gun. In the first wave of consolidation after the abolition of the fiefdoms in 1871 it became Myōdō Prefecture, which included the Awa region and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island">Awaji Island</a>. In 1873 it also took in the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>. In the second wave of consolidation the Sanuki region separated in 1875 to form Kagawa; in 1876 Awaji Island passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyogo-prefecture">Hyōgo Prefecture</a> and the Awa region became part of Kōchi Prefecture. On 2 March 1880 the prefecture separated from Kōchi and was inaugurated as Tokushima Prefecture.</p><p>In August 1945 the Washi no Mon gate of Tokushima Castle was destroyed by fire at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The Nankai earthquake of December 1946 caused vast damage throughout the prefecture. Tokushima University was created in 1949, and Naruto, Komatsushima and Anan were inaugurated as cities between 1947 and 1958. Takeo Miki, who was born in Tokushima, was elected Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in October 1974. The Ōnaruto Bridge to Hyōgo was completed in 1985, and the Akashi Kaikyō Bridge in 1998 connected Tokushima with the mainland by road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture had a population of 682,439 in February 2025. It depends on agriculture, forestry and fisheries, and Japanese indigo is produced only here. Its produce, including Naruto sweet potatoes, sudachi citrus, lotus roots and strawberries, goes mainly to the Kansai region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Among its cultural sites are Tokushima Castle, the Iya Valley, the Oboke Koboke Gorge, the Naruto whirlpools, the Otsuka Museum of Art and the Awa Marionette Jōruri tradition of puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Its sports teams include the football club Tokushima Vortis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokushima Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A violent dispute with the Inada family of Awaji in 1870 led to Awaji's transfer, and after abolition in 1871 Awa became <b>Tokushima Prefecture</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/tokushima-domain">Tokushima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
